WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.564
ANDY RUDD: Hello, and welcome.

00:00:06.564 --> 00:00:08.189
We're excited to have
you join us today

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.720
where we'll be sharing some
exciting ideas around enhancing

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.110
collaboration with Sheets,
Python, and Google Cloud

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.140
Platform.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.200
I'm Andy Rudd, a product manager
with G Suite currently focused

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.670
on Google Sheets.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.710
I'm joined today by
my colleague Anil

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.080
who is also a product manager
working on Google Cloud

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.838
Platform.

00:00:26.838 --> 00:00:28.880
Anil and I and our teams
have been thinking a lot

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.950
about analytical collaboration.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.980
And for many reasons,
collaboration is more important

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.940
now than it's ever been.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.040
Sometimes, though,
and in particular when

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.100
it comes to complex
analysis, today's solutions

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.260
can be a bit tedious.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.890
Often, this is because the
tools that we use and prefer

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:50.760
varies significantly from
job role to job role.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.210
We've been working towards
more of an ideal solution.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.540
And we're excited to give you
a sneak peek of that today.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.550
From our point of view, an ideal
solution would do a few things.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.690
It would allow anyone
in an organization

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.570
to be self-service to be
able to do the analysis they

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.350
need to do, regardless of
their technical proficiency.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.340
It would allow common
analytical tasks and workflows

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.730
that a company uses
internally to be standardized.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.910
And it might even allow the
most novices spreadsheet users

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.940
to leverage powerful
open source libraries

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.145
and cloud infrastructure
to get stuff done.

00:01:27.145 --> 00:01:29.270
So with that said, the
solution that we'll show off

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.850
today would allow you to expose
Python-based custom functions

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.690
in Google Sheets.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.000
These custom functions
are powered by GCP

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.040
serverless platform
product, Cloud Run.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.200
With the serverless
model, users would not

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.130
have to deal with
managing infrastructure.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:48.028
And they would only pay when
their functions are run.

00:01:48.028 --> 00:01:49.570
So with that said,
today, we're going

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.460
to go through a few things.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:56.130
What happens now will cover a
common analytical frustration

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.260
that we hear about in
companies all around the world.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:02.360
We'll talk about
what could happen.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.170
We'll show you a demo of
that tool actually in use.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.670
And then we'll give
you a sneak peek

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.270
under the hood in the form of an
architectural overview of what

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.720
we've built so far.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.400
And then we'll
give you some tips

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.010
for staying in the loop
to learn about what

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.710
we're up to, as we continue to
develop this product further.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.660
So what happens today?

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.980
Let's take a look
at how collaboration

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.950
happens in companies all
around the world today.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.070
Let's enter an
alternate universe

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.910
if you'll allow us where Anil
and I are still colleagues.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.180
But this time, we work at
a company called Altostrat.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.490
Altostrat is a
packaged foods company

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.700
that specializes in sweet
snacks in particular.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.820
Andy is a business
analyst there.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.940
And he is often responsible
for aggregating key business

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.490
metrics, delivering
presentations

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:59.900
to leadership to give you
the latest updates on KPIs.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.060
Anil is a data scientist.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.160
He has a background
in statistics.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.260
And he is most
comfortable/happiest when

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.900
he is programming in Python.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.490
They have worked on some
projects over the years.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.700
But they do not know
each other that well.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.700
It's often been more of a
give than a take with Andy

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.000
firing off last-minute requests
for some late breaking analysis

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.060
that leadership has asked for.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.380
They were both comfortable
with G Suite communication

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.500
tools, chat, and email.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.310
But Andy is more comfortable
in a spreadsheet.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.330
He's familiar with the
traditional productivity

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.030
suite of tools.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.290
Anil is familiar with
cloud infrastructure.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:43.018
He likes to reprogram in IDD,
the code editor of his choice.

00:03:43.018 --> 00:03:43.810
And one more thing.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.660
Lately, Altostrat has been
thinking about how they could

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.870
re-evaluate their existing
suppliers of two key

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.940
ingredients-- sugar and flour--

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.640
unsurprising, based on
what they're known for--

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.920
and how they might consider
other suppliers in order

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.680
to keep their costs
down and maybe also be

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.140
more flexible and responding
to demand planning

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.800
exercises that come
up in the future.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.760
So let's go inside a
recent Friday morning

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.350
when Andy and Anil's
paths crossed.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:15.650
This will all start when
Ellen, who manages the Business

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.660
Analysis Unit at
Altostrat, is thinking

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.899
about what needs to be prepared
for an upcoming leadership

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:21.420
review.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.570
So let's say the next Monday--

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.100
that's one business day away--

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.960
there is a 2, 3 wrap
up report that's

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.640
being delivered to Altostrat's
executive leadership.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.950
Ellen and Andy have
talked about, offline,

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.250
some of the things that
might go into that report.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.917
And they've determined
that he should be

00:04:37.917 --> 00:04:39.860
responsible for a few things.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.260
Summarizing last quarter
sales, that sort of always

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.090
implicit in this report.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:47.930
But also doing analysis around
this discussion about supplier

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:48.890
analysis.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.102
So looking at the
suppliers that exist now,

00:04:52.102 --> 00:04:53.810
looking at other
suppliers in the market,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.570
which Ellen and Andy have
collected some data on.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.360
And then looking at how
the various output in price

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.930
of those two key ingredients
could affect Altostrat's bottom

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.130
line.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.800
There's many downstream
implications of this decision.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.030
It will go into their overall
production capacity planning.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.360
It will help them have
better revenue estimates.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.110
And it may even feed
into their price

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.020
modeling for their
products in the future.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.360
So Ellen kicks us
off with an email.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.840
She says, Andy, on Monday,
we've got this presentation.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.270
Let's make sure that we
deliver that supplier analysis

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.000
we talked about.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.420
I'd like you to reach out
to the data science team

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.770
and get that model.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.317
Andy says, of course, I'm on it.

00:05:33.317 --> 00:05:35.900
So it looks like he's going to
take some of the inputs they've

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.150
collected, work with
the data science team

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.170
to revise a model that maybe
they've done in the past,

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:44.840
and then update it with new
supplier capacity information.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.370
So Andy knows who to go to and
he has a question like this.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:50.570
He reached out to Anil.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.930
And he says, hey, it
is that time again.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:54.980
Lucky you.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.295
Can we please remodel
Altostrat's output,

00:05:57.295 --> 00:05:58.670
given some of the
new information

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:02.120
on suppliers that
we've collected?

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:02.620
Also, sure.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:03.120
Of course.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.990
I think I sort of
remember us doing

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.402
something similar to this a
quarter or a few quarters ago.

00:06:08.402 --> 00:06:10.360
Let me try to find that
model that I developed.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.660
And we'll run it with the new
inputs that you have attached.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:17.110
So maybe Anil's not
expecting this request today.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.100
But he's going to
make time for it.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.940
He'll have to find this model
that he's written in the past

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:25.558
and update it with
new information.

00:06:25.558 --> 00:06:27.100
Before he digs in,
though, he's going

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.110
to check in with his team to
baseline on other requests

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.150
that they have on
their plate today.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:35.530
So Greta is another data
scientist at Altostrat.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:36.680
Anil messages, her.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.210
And he says a little gloomily,
hey, another request just

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.230
came in from the
Business Analysis Team.

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:45.170
Another one, she says.

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:47.890
What are they looking
for this time?

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:50.080
Anil says, the
product optimization

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.080
with new suppliers.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.770
Greta remembers Anil walking her
through this model in the past.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.310
And she says, hey,
you know what.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.030
Let me take on the FP&amp;A
request that we also

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.490
have going on our plate today.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:03.570
And you enjoy this one.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:05.530
Take it on yourself.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.600
Anil is a little
frustrated here, maybe.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.410
But we know there's
a lot on his plate.

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.680
But he's going to
get the job done.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:15.210
So first things first.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.970
Anil starts to look
in his local computer

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:22.033
to determine when he did
this analysis previously.

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:23.200
He's going to track it down.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.700
It's always a
little bit tedious.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:28.510
He has to think about when
the last time a question

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.720
or request like this came in.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:31.870
What he named the model.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.750
What he named the file,
including the Python code when

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:36.500
he last developed this.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.950
It's always not quite as
humorless as it should be.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:43.050
So that's fine and well.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:46.970
But, unfortunately, a little
while later, Andy realizes

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.400
he left out some key inputs.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.560
This time to get Anil's
attention more quickly,

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:52.630
he sends a chat.

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:56.150
He says, hey, I
forgot one more thing.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:58.010
Anil's starting to respond.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:07:59.330
The frustration is mounting.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:01.080
Let's see what
this is all about.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.710
Andy says, hey, I left
out our current supplier.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.300
What a stupid silly mistake.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.740
And so I've just passed along
updated capacity information

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:10.634
to you.

00:08:10.634 --> 00:08:12.740
Anil says, of course,
hey, that's fine.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:13.800
No worries.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.720
I'll be sure to
include those as well.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:16.960
So what does this mean?

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.890
Anil still has a few morning
meetings to get through.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:21.590
But once he gets
back to the model,

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.140
he'll turn his attention
to Andy's request.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:27.020
He'll think about combining the
now two disparate inputs that

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:30.320
have been provided and then
providing his results back

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:33.600
to Andy in some fashion.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.590
So Andy's asked for
a few things before.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.400
But Anil has now found
the model that we

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.483
care about this time around.

00:08:40.483 --> 00:08:41.400
What do we want to do?

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.540
We want to determine Altostrat's
potential product output, given

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.320
a number of different suppliers
with different information.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.730
They can provide key ingredients
in different quantities

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:51.980
and prices.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.020
We get a sneak peak here of the
model that Anil has developed.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:55.970
It looks pretty cool.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.790
There's some awesome
things happening.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:01.950
He's using some popular Python
libraries, like SciPy and NumPy

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:04.440
to do this optimization.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.820
Anil's working in the local
code editor of his choice.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:09.500
He might tweak some
of the logic as he

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.990
goes to make improvements
that are easy.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.670
He'll swap in the
new inputs that

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.770
have been provided by Andy.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:19.820
And then, unfortunately, Andy
is very scared, very intimidated

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:21.930
by any sign of code whatsoever.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.080
So he can't just share
his Python notebook.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.420
But what he'll do
instead is once he's

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.520
happy with the results
that have been generated,

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:33.940
he'll output a CSV file that
can be more easily consumed.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:37.240
With that done, Anil will
turn to Google Sheets,

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:42.130
which is a well-understood tool
medium often used at Altostrat.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.040
An innovative, collaborative
productivity, Sheets

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:48.070
is known as a great way
to share an analysis

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:49.840
throughout the company.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:52.620
So a while later, Anil's
happy with his results.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.620
He imports that CSV we talked
about into Google Sheets,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.580
and he shares it away.

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:01.310
He wants to give Andy an update.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:02.570
So he sends a message.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:06.100
He says, hey, I just shared you
a spreadsheet with the results.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:06.880
Some time passes.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:08.530
But Andy says, oh,
thank you so much.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.670
We're well on our way.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:12.880
Is there anything interesting?

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.050
And here, Anil's
saying, that's really

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.380
where your expertise comes in.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:19.580
You tell me, we've generated
some cool business insights.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.185
What I care about
is I made my model

00:10:21.185 --> 00:10:23.782
a little better along the way.

00:10:23.782 --> 00:10:25.490
Andy doesn't have time
to look right now.

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.710
But based on his work
with Anil in the past,

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:30.110
he trusts that the
outputs here are sound.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:35.550
So a few hours have passed.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.050
Andy's been a bit distracted.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.930
But results at hand, he
shares the output with them.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.410
He fires off an email.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:44.160
Hey, the data scientist
team turned around

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:45.870
that analysis we asked for.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:47.160
Optimization is herein.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.500
I have linked to you a Google
Sheet containing the results.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.480
What do you think?

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.296
Ellen says, that looks good.

00:10:54.296 --> 00:10:55.340
But you know what.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:56.590
I've been thinking about this.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.740
And often in the past,
our executive team

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:01.750
has asked them
questions on the fly.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.730
They've wanted to
know maybe, what

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.220
if we got our suppliers
to reduce their proposed

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:07.750
prices by 10%?

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.770
Let's plan for that
scenario as well.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.990
That's a reasonable request.

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.330
But it's going to shift
Andy into a different gear.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:16.830
It's getting late.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:19.260
Remember, the aim was
to wrap up this analysis

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:20.910
by the end of the day.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:25.580
So rapidly, he writes
another email to Anil.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:27.520
I hate to do this on
a Friday afternoon.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.070
We've all gotten this message.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:30.360
But I need a minor tweak.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:36.190
Can we rerun that model
assuming a 10% price reduction?

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.628
Hearing nothing, he chats
Anil a few minutes later.

00:11:39.628 --> 00:11:41.170
He says, hey, I just
wanted to check.

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.390
Did you see my email?

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.092
Still nothing-- another ping.

