And you can see that I
get some autocompletions.

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:49.530
So I click this.

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.660
And it looks like it needs
the amount of flour available.

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:55.500
So that's available
in this cell.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:58.270
Next, it needs the amount
of sugar available.

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:00.300
So let's go to this cell.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.360
And I can go ahead and
invoke this function.

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:06.630
What this is now doing is
calling my Python code,

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:09.420
running my model,
and returning result.

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:12.740
You can see here that based
on this quantity of flour

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:18.280
and sugar, my model just
turned these values.

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:21.600
Now, this is great because I can
call it directly from a sheet.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.810
And because I'm in a sheet,
I can do all the cool things

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.190
I typically do in a sheet.

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:30.210
For example, I can erase
these hardcoded values.

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:33.360
And I can simply
drag my formula up.

00:24:33.360 --> 00:24:36.240
And you can see that it will
do the same computation calling

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:39.090
my Python code
this time instead.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:42.195
I can also finish answering
the rest of the question

00:24:42.195 --> 00:24:45.650
that Andy is posturing
by dragging down.

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:47.940
And you can see here
that we have populated

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:50.520
entire table of results.

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:54.150
You could also imagine that
if Andy comes again and wants

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:56.860
to add a new supplier--

00:24:56.860 --> 00:24:58.920
let's say supplier nine.

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:02.820
And that supplier can produce,
let's say, 600 units of flour

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:04.790
and 200 years of sugar.

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:07.680
And he can simply
copy down this formula

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:10.450
and have this invoke
the same Python code

00:25:10.450 --> 00:25:14.680
and model to come up with a
new forecast of production.

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:17.240
You can see that right here.

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:18.990
Because we're in
Sheets, we can also

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.820
take advantage of the rest
of Sheets functionality.

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:24.390
For instance, let's say
I wanted to figure out

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:28.630
which supplier is going to
give us the most revenue.

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:31.990
Well, I can merely
ask Sheets about that.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:37.890
So I can say, supplier
with maximum revenue.

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:39.720
You can see here that
Sheets is telling us

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:42.210
that that supplier is going
to be supplier number four.

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:47.070
And it optimizes
revenue at $17,750.

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:47.700
Great.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:51.490
We've just seen how this
workflow looks in action.

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:55.490
Let's take a look now behind
the scenes to see how it works.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:57.010
There are two scenarios here.

00:25:57.010 --> 00:26:00.330
One is I as a developer wanting
to expose these functions

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:02.880
to Andy in Sheets.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:04.650
Let's see how that works.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:10.560
I start by creating my Python
code locally on my machine.

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:12.560
And I use the tool
we've created,

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:18.042
Sheet CF, to deploy that
package into Google Cloud.

00:26:18.042 --> 00:26:20.000
Well, what's really
happening behind the scenes

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:22.340
is we are creating a
container image that

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:26.030
will get deployed into Google
Cloud Platform's serverless

00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:28.790
product Cloud Run.

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:31.950
So we have some source
code on my machine.

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:34.350
And we have Cloud Run,
which expects a container.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:37.220
So we're going to have to figure
out a way to build a container.

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:39.362
That's actually
what Sheet CF does.

00:26:39.362 --> 00:26:40.820
First thing it
does, actually, when

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:45.050
you run it is create a container
image using Google Cloud's

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.550
manage servers
called Cloud Build.

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:51.080
Cloud Build takes a
description of a set of tasks

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:54.800
and a formal of how to create a
container image as some source

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:55.880
code.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.050
It executes those tasks and
stores those container images

00:27:00.050 --> 00:27:02.610
into Google's
Container Registry.

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:06.050
So this is automated
with a Sheet CF deploy.

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:06.650
Great.

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:09.690
So now we've gone from
source code to a container.

00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:12.560
And that container now lives
in the Container Registry.

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:16.010
The next step is to create
a deployment in Cloud

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:18.060
Run of that very container.

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:19.560
So that's what happens next.

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:21.650
When Cloud Run
gets that request,

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:23.480
it goes to the
Container Registry,

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:26.950
pulls down the container,
runs it locally,

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:31.540
and exposes an end
point on the internet.

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:34.480
Finally, we secure that end
point so only the G Suite

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:36.360
infrastructure can invoke it.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:41.614
And we expose an add-on for Andy
to be used inside of Sheets.

00:27:41.614 --> 00:27:42.590
So great.

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:44.638
That's how it looks like
for me as an author.

00:27:44.638 --> 00:27:46.430
Now, what's happening
when Andy is actually

00:27:46.430 --> 00:27:48.980
invoking these
functions on his machine

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:51.050
inside of Google Sheets?

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:53.160
So that workflow
looks like this.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.352
We start with Andy
using Google Sheets.

00:27:55.352 --> 00:27:56.810
And, of course,
since we've already

00:27:56.810 --> 00:27:58.970
deployed this end
point in Cloud Run,

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:02.480
we have that running in GCP.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:05.750
So Andy fires up a
sheet, picks up a cell,

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:08.180
and enters a
function invocation.

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:11.300
In this case, he's calling the
optimize production function.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:13.520
What this does
behind the scenes is

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:15.950
the Sheets knows that
this function is actually

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:19.310
implemented by a
Cloud Run endpoint.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:21.350
It goes ahead and
takes that information,

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:23.900
converts it into
an HTTP request,

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:26.360
sends that over to Cloud Run.

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:30.200
Cloud Run then looks at my
container and executes it.

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:33.380
As we saw, the source code
running in my container

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:38.000
was a series of Python that
use a cloud SQL and cloud

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.940
storage to gather his results.

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:43.490
Once my function
is done executing,

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:47.410
it returns those results in
the form of an HTTP response.

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:49.620
Sheets then takes
that and shares

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:53.920
that with Andy in the form of
displaying it inside his sheet.

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:57.020
So you can see here the power of
Google Cloud Platform is masked

00:28:57.020 --> 00:28:59.120
completely behind the scenes.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:02.120
And all Andy needs
to see is Sheets.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:04.190
But my code running
in Google Cloud

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:06.820
has been containerized
for me automatically.

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:10.430
And in doing so, I have access
to a variety of cloudy PIs

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:13.610
and the full power Python.

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.650
So what can you do with
this type of thing?

00:29:15.650 --> 00:29:17.373
Well, we see several
key use cases.

00:29:17.373 --> 00:29:19.290
The first is the one
we've been talking about,

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:22.050
which is collaborating
across teams and rules.

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:23.540
Andy is on the business side.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:25.460
I'm a data scientist.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:27.470
Sure, we used to
use Sheets already.

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:29.330
But I was really copying
and pasting results

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:31.820
and maybe loading up
a CSV occasionally.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:35.360
Now, in this new workflow,
we can actually collaborate

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:38.540
in a self-service fashion.

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:41.970
The next is exposing
proprietary calculations.

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:44.190
How many times have
we all seen situations

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:47.010
where we take a spreadsheet
with some calculations.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:50.100
We make a copy of it to tweak
some of those calculations.

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:55.307
And all of a sudden, we lose
our single record of truth.

00:29:55.307 --> 00:29:57.390
Imagine this happening
throughout your enterprise.

00:29:57.390 --> 00:30:00.810
Imagine how many variations
of some basic calculation

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:03.360
are now running around
in your organization.

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:04.980
Well, with this
kind of model where

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:09.150
you centralize the functions
inside of a Python module

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.240
and expose that
through these atoms,

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:14.670
the business can
continue maintaining

00:30:14.670 --> 00:30:17.760
the integrity of
those calculations

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:20.950
and update them
whenever they're needed.

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:22.870
What ends up happening
is the business

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:25.670
will always pick up the
latest and greatest results.

00:30:25.670 --> 00:30:29.200
And there's no worry
about spreadsheet drift.

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:32.950
Another is accessing
multiple custom data sources.

00:30:32.950 --> 00:30:34.820
We already saw this
with my Python code,

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:38.740
which was using cloud
storage as well as cloud SQL.

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:42.070
You might have custom
other data sources

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:44.777
that might be on GitHub
or other locations.

00:30:44.777 --> 00:30:47.110
And because we're running in
a containerized environment

00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:51.335
with Cloud Run, you have
access to all of them.

00:30:51.335 --> 00:30:54.290
Another is calling cloud APIs.

00:30:54.290 --> 00:30:58.400
Because most Google Cloud APIs
have a robust client library,

00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:01.040
it becomes very, very
easy to consume them

00:31:01.040 --> 00:31:02.760
from my Python code.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:06.740
And the fact that I can marry
the results of that into Sheets

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:09.210
makes this a very, very
compelling situation.

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:11.780
For example, I might
use Google Cloud

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:14.120
to translate APIs
or machine learning

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:16.130
APIs to autotrain a model.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:18.260
And all of a sudden, I
can stitch those together

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:23.110
and expose them to the business
in the form of a spreadsheet.

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:25.460
And finally, Python
is what's powering

00:31:25.460 --> 00:31:26.920
all of this under the hood.

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:28.910
And Python, as you
might be aware,

00:31:28.910 --> 00:31:32.240
is full of hundreds of
great complex packages that

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:34.940
make sure that you do not
have to start from scratch.

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:39.980
In fact, I use NumPy and SciPy
to do a linear programming

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:42.180
system inside of my code.

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:46.580
It was very easy to do in
under 100 lines of code.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:47.340
So great.

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:49.110
What have we seen today so far?

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:52.080
We've seen that in today's
world, collaborating

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:54.480
across the business,
across experts,

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:56.820
regular organization
is a challenge.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:59.686
And Sheets already
plays a key role.

00:31:59.686 --> 00:32:02.920
The opportunity we
see is for Sheets

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:05.980
to play an even greater
role and for the business

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:11.560
to be able to expose functions
to the rest of the organization

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:13.600
through some
standardized interfaces.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:15.670
And what that means
is folks like Andy

00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:19.210
can self-service
those calculations.

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:21.610
And I can spend
more time focusing

00:32:21.610 --> 00:32:24.190
on my nights and weekends.

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:26.800
We're excited about
this approach.

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:29.440
And we welcome you to
learn more about it

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:31.300
and try it out for yourself.

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:33.640
You can do so by signing
up on this form link

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:37.840
below where you'll get notified
when this was made available.

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:40.960
We're really excited to hear
what you might do with this

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:42.790
and hear the kinds
of scenarios that

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:46.030
might be applicable
in your organization.

00:32:46.030 --> 00:32:48.880
And on behalf of Andy,
we'd like to thank you

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:51.690
for watching this presentation.

